course: dictionary
lesson: 11573
title: Strong feelings between people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Animosity is a noun. It means a strong feeling of dislike or anger towards someone, and it often lasts a long time. You can feel animosity towards a person, or there can be animosity between two families, teams or countries.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «There was animosity between the two rival teams for years.» The words rival and for years show that this is not a quick, passing dislike. It is a strong feeling that has lasted.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in: «There was strong dislike between the two rival teams for years.» It still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: Animosity means a strong feeling of dislike or anger towards someone.
fact: Animosity often lasts a long time, so it is stronger than a quick argument.
fact: «Animosity between» is a common phrase in stories, history and the news.
fact: Animosity is usually an uncountable noun, so you say «some animosity», not «an animosity».
fact: Hostility is a close synonym: it means a similar unfriendly, angry feeling.
exercise: «The two rival gangs had felt animosity for years.» What does animosity mean? | Friendship and trust | *Strong, long-lasting dislike | Tiredness after a long walk | A small, quick laugh
exercise: Lena says, «There is no animosity between my cousin and me.» What does this mean? | They were enemies who hated each other | They lost touch years ago | They are best friends who share everything | *They do not dislike each other at all
exercise: Why might an author use animosity instead of just dislike? | It means happy | It is the name of a town | *It sounds stronger, and it suggests the feeling has lasted a long time | It is a shorter, easier word
test: «The animosity between the villages led to a long war.» What does animosity mean? | A kind of weather | Shared happiness | Being very tired | *Strong dislike that lasts a long time
test: Which sentence shows animosity? | The two kings laughed at the same joke | The two kings went hunting together every spring | *The two kings had hated each other for years | The two kings shared a cup of tea
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to animosity? | Friendship | *Hostility | Curiosity | Happiness
